Applicable upstream ASF bounds, count and enumeration fixes adapted to 1.13.1.
Include the private QWORD-size prerequisite without changing exported APIs.
Use long for existing 1.x file offsets; bounds are checked before additions.
Include the upstream follow-up that skips unknown attribute payloads and the
post-2.3.2 header-size correction required before save-time subtraction.
Upstream commits: https://github.com/taglib/taglib/commit/<full ID below>.

From 57c8dbe014623ff0b04bb46100ea5aff1646cd87 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: "Stephen F. Booth" <me@sbooth.org>
Date: Sun, 17 Dec 2023 16:49:16 -0600
Subject: [PATCH] Use long long (QWORD) for sizes


From 04993427d7056c70df24f6350384df5362afa755 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Acts1631 <69813585+acts-1631@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sun, 2 Aug 2026 02:23:08 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] Bound ASF attribute parsing to object data (#1389)

A truncated ASF attribute object could declare a large count and make
the parser create empty attributes after reaching the end of its data.

Validate each attribute object's data extent and stop parsing when an
attribute would exceed it.

From 2ada48a77f9b5158f6a4313ed7a90de8efc4eb75 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Acts1631 <69813585+acts-1631@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Mon, 3 Aug 2026 12:46:01 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] ASF: limit header object count (#1395)

ASF header parsing retained an unbounded number of objects. A crafted
file with many small objects could consume disproportionate memory.

Reject files whose header object count exceeds the parser limit.

From fa4c623ab17dfee5fc92b003eec0888fc2682713 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Acts1631 <69813585+acts-1631@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Tue, 18 Aug 2026 15:27:26 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] Bound ASF header-extension child parsing (#1426)

ASF header extensions trusted their nested data size and accepted
zero-sized child objects. A crafted file could make parsing continue
outside the enclosing object while retaining one object per child
header, exhausting process memory when opened.

Bound nested data to the enclosing object, require a complete child
header, and cap the nested object count.

